1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This bug is marked Low. It is quietly blocking US$480,000 in open deals.
Nobody on the team knows.

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Stakes connects Jira to customers, deals, support requests,
conversations and goals through the Teamwork Graph.

3
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:50,000
It turns that evidence into a score and ranks the backlog,
so this silent fire is impossible to miss.

4
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:02,500
Every number has a source. Six tiles show how revenue, customers,
support, conversations, goals and urgency contribute.

5
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:15,000
AI explains only the evidence. It never calculates the score,
and unsupported numbers are rejected.

6
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:35,000
A person confirms each priority change.
Stakes records the evidence and approver,
then plans a sprint within the team's capacity.

7
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:47,000
When the fix ships, Stakes prepares updates
for account owners and support agents.

8
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Nothing goes out automatically. Review the drafts, select Send,
and the optional bridge delivers the internal note to Slack.

9
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Ask Stakes Analyst what to fix first, what a customer is waiting on,
or what a release unblocks.

10
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The same read-only tools work through MCP in an external AI assistant,
with the user's permissions and source links.

11
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:45,000
A Confluence digest and macro carry the same context into planning.
A read-only Jira field makes the score searchable with JQL.

12
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Connector in. Graph queries out. Deterministic scoring,
guarded Forge LLMs, and a no-egress core.
Stakes: know what's riding on every work item.
